Stats Data
----------

For package layout, building, tests, and a map of major source modules, see **README.md**
in this directory.

The on-disk stats format (archive files, dumpfiles, DEBUG /dev/shm captures, RabbitMQ
payloads) is documented in **docs/RAW_DATA_FORMAT.md**: every line kind, header and schema
fields, sample rows, value sentinels, fast/full tiers, shared-node marks, and the Python reader
in `python/hpcperfstats_raw`. Every stats type and key the daemon can emit, with its meaning,
unit and counter/gauge kind, is in **docs/METRICS_REFERENCE.md** (generated by
`scripts/gen_metrics_reference.py`).

### x86 counters and RAPL (direct perf_event)

On x86 the monitor reads CPU performance counters, memory-controller uncore counters and
**RAPL energy** with its own direct `perf_event` readers; no third-party PMU library is
built or linked on any architecture (LIKWID was removed in 3.1 Phases 2+3). `x86_core_perf` opens the core events per CPU for
`host_cpu_hw` (published by `cpu_counter_metrics`), `x86_uncore_perf` reads the sysfs
`uncore_imc_<N>`, `uncore_hbm_<N>` and `amd_df` PMUs (each row sums the CPUs in the
PMU `cpumask`, so all sockets), and `x86_rapl` reads the perf `power` PMU with a per-domain
powercap (`energy_uj`) fallback. Events are never pinned (reads are scaled by enabled /
running time) and the monitor never reads or writes MSRs.

Configure selects the CPU counter backend with `--with-cpu-counter-backend={auto|x86|arm}`:
`auto` (default) is `x86` on x86 hosts and `arm` elsewhere, and `x86` is rejected on
non-x86 hosts. The CPU vendor (Intel or AMD) is read at runtime; there is no compile-time
vendor switch. The capability slug carries `x86perf` for the x86 backend (for example
`x86_64-ver3.1-hw-ib-nvgpu-intelgpu-lustre-beegfs-x86perf-sent1-slowtier1`). README.md
("x86 counters") has the design and the upgrade notes for 3.1 Phases 2+3; LIKWID (3.0) history
is in `LIKWID_MIGRATION.md`.

**Static linking:** default configure uses `--enable-all-static` (see
`configure --help`). The static bundle builds only `libev` and `librabbitmq` (rabbitmq-c),
plus `libbpf` for the eBPF metric profiler; they must be on the linker path. A
workspace-oriented flow is `monitor/scripts/ensure_dotbuild_prefix_static.sh`
(followed by configuring with `CPPFLAGS`/`LDFLAGS` pointing at that prefix); see
`monitor/scripts/build_static_bundle.sh` (`STATIC_PIN_*`) for pinned versions and options.

